Project 8 
Creating a VPC Peering Connection between two VPCs 
note : VPC Peering is a one to one networking connection between two VPCs that enables you to route the traffic between them privately . So every instance in the two VPCs can communicate with each other 
other note : you can create two VPC by reading the steps of project 3 
Task 1 : so Let's create VPC Peering 
1 - Inside the console search about VPC then choose Peering connections in the left then choose Create Peering connection
Name - optional : Lab-Peer
VPC ID (Requester): Lab VPC
VPC ID ( Accepter ) : Shared VPC 
then Create Peering connection
note : if you make a VPC peering connection you must wait for the acceptance of the other VPC owner  but in this project I am the owner of the Two VPCs so let's accept the request of making the peering the connection 
2 - choose Actions then choose Accept request 
Task 2 : configuring the route tables between the two VPCs 
3 - in the left choose Route Tables then choose Lab Public Route Table for Lab VPC then open Routes section then choose Edit routes then choose Add route  
Destination : 10.5.0.0/16
Target : peering connection  then choose pcx-............... which contains lab peer then save changes 
4 - get back to the Route tables and choose Shared-VPC Route Table then open routes tab then edit route then add route ( same steps ) 
Destination : 10.0.0.0/16
Target : Peering connection then choose the value which is Lab peer 
then save the changes 
Task 3 : Enabling VPC Flow logs to provide insight on the data moving across the network 
I setup VPC Flow Logs to monitor the network traffic moving between networks 
5 - on the left choose Your VPCs then choose Shared VPC then open Flow Logs section then choose Create Flow Log 
Name - optional : SharedVPCLogs
Maximum aggregation interval : 1 minute 
Destination : send to CloudWatch logs 
Destination log group : ShareVPCFlowLogs
IAM Role : vpc-flow-logs-Role 
Choose Create flow log 
6 - In your VPCs , choose Shared VPC then open flow tags then open the link which existed under Destination name  to see the cloudwatch log group that was created 
